saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11c · Step 3 – What do similar cases say? (Full-text search → vector search)\n\nJonas has described his symptoms: twisted, swollen, a \"Plopp\", wobbly on the steps. More than a thousand people described their complaints before him, and their diagnoses are known. What did knees like his turn out to be? We ask twice: once by keyword, once by meaning.\n\n**Question:** Which earlier cases are similar to Jonas' report, and what did they turn out to be?\n\n- **Features:** Oracle Text (`CONTAINS`, `SCORE`, `CTX_DOC.SNIPPET`, fuzzy, stemming, `NEAR`), AI Vector Search (`VECTOR_EMBEDDING`, `VECTOR_DISTANCE`, in-database ONNX model)\n- **Pattern:** M1 – find, then check (part 1: find)\n- **Data:** `SYMPTOM_REPORTS` (1,458 FHIR symptom reports of other persons plus Jonas' app report), `DIAGNOSES`, `MEDICATION_RECOMMENDATIONS`\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `01_run_all.sql` (text index from `06_create_text_index.sql`, embeddings from `05_create_embeddings.sql`). Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\nBased on the blog posts [Oracle Text search in Autonomous Database](https://blogs.oracle.com/coretec/oracle-text-search-in-autonomous-database) and [Getting started with vectors in 23ai](https://blogs.oracle.com/coretec/getting-started-with-vectors-in-23ai). Jonas' report has the ID `SR-2026-11-16-0001`; the other reports come from the FHIR records (`SOURCE = 'fhir'`).\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. The prepared indexes\n\nTwo searches are prepared on the symptom reports: a German full-text index and an embedding for every report, calculated in the database.\n\n**Question:** Are both indexes ready?\n\n**How the query answers it:** **Full text:** the `CONTEXT` index `SYMPTOM_REPORTS_TXT_IDX` on `REPORT_TEXT` is configured for German: compound splitting (*Plopp-Geräusch* → *Plopp*, *Geräusch*), German stemming and fuzzy matching, sync on commit.\n\n**Vector:** every report has an embedding in `REPORT_EMBEDDING`, calculated in the database by the ONNX model `ALL_MINILM_L12_V2` (384 dimensions, `FLOAT32`). `COUNT` on a `VECTOR` column is not allowed, so the check uses `CASE … IS NOT NULL`.\n\n**Result:** the text index is ready (`INDEXED`) and covers all 1,459 reports – the 1,458 reports of other persons plus Jonas' app report. Every report has an embedding with 384 dimensions in `FLOAT32`. Both searches work on the same data."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT idx_name,\n       idx_table,\n       idx_text_name,\n       idx_status,\n       idx_docid_count\nFROM   ctx_user_indexes\nWHERE  idx_name = 'SYMPTOM_REPORTS_TXT_IDX';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT COUNT(*)                                                    AS reports,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN report_embedding IS NOT NULL THEN 1 END)    AS with_embedding,\n       MAX(VECTOR_DIMENSION_COUNT(report_embedding))               AS dimensions,\n       MAX(VECTOR_DIMENSION_FORMAT(report_embedding))              AS dimension_format\nFROM   symptom_reports;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. Jonas' report\n\nBoth searches start from Jonas' own words.\n\n**Question:** What exactly did Jonas write?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The free text from the app (see 11a) was loaded into `SYMPTOM_REPORTS` together with the structured answers. This text is the question for both searches.\n\n**Result:** the same report as in 11a, now one row among the 1,459 reports: Jonas describes a twist, a \"Plopp\", swelling, pain when walking and an unstable knee. The structured answers say the same in fields (twist, pop, instability, pain 6 of 10) – but the search only uses the free text, like a person typing into a search box."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT report_id,\n       source,\n       reported_on,\n       report_text,\n       mechanism,\n       audible_pop,\n       swelling_onset_hours,\n       instability,\n       pain_nrs\nFROM   symptom_reports\nWHERE  report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Keyword search: \"Plopp\"\n\nWe start with the word that stands out in Jonas' report: \"Plopp\".\n\n**Question:** Which earlier reports contain the word \"Plopp\"?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`CONTAINS(report_text, 'Plopp', 1) > 0` uses the text index; `SCORE(1)` returns the relevance of the `CONTAINS` call with the label 1. Words that occur in few documents score higher.\n\n**Result:** only 12 of 1,458 reports contain the word, all with the same score. 7 of them turned out to be ruptures of the anterior cruciate ligament (*Vordere Kreuzbandruptur*), all confirmed by MRI. The other 5 – four sprains (*Kniedistorsion*) and one report without a diagnosis – say *\"Kein Plopp, nichts Auffälliges\"* (no pop, nothing unusual). Full text finds words, not negations: almost every second hit says the opposite of what we searched for."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT s.report_id,\n       SCORE(1)            AS score,\n       d.diagnosis_label,\n       d.confirmed_by,\n       s.report_text\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\nAND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Plopp', 1) > 0\nORDER  BY score DESC, s.report_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### What did the \"Plopp\" cases turn out to be?\n\n**Question:** What were the \"Plopp\" cases diagnosed as?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The same hits grouped by diagnosis. `RATIO_TO_REPORT` gives the share of each diagnosis.\n\n**Result:** 58 % cruciate ligament ruptures (7), 33 % sprains (4), 8 % without a diagnosis (1). By keyword, Jonas' knee looks like a cruciate ligament rupture – but 5 of the 12 hits are texts that deny the very word."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT d.diagnosis_label,\n       COUNT(*)                                        AS reports,\n       ROUND(RATIO_TO_REPORT(COUNT(*)) OVER () * 100)  AS share_pct\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\nAND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Plopp') > 0\nGROUP  BY d.diagnosis_label\nORDER  BY reports DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. Show the hit in context: `CTX_DOC.SNIPPET`\n\nA list of IDs convinces nobody; a doctor wants to read the words in context.\n\n**Question:** Where in each text is the hit?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`CTX_DOC.SNIPPET` returns the text passage around the match, with the search term in `<b>` tags. The second argument identifies the row; with `SET_KEY_TYPE('PRIMARY_KEY')` this is the primary key `REPORT_ID` (the default is the `ROWID`). The setting applies to the session.\n\n**Result:** the snippet shows each hit with its neighbouring words, so the negation becomes visible at a glance: *\"Dabei hat es **Plopp** gemacht\"* (it went pop) next to *\"Kein **Plopp**, nichts Auffälliges\"* (no pop, nothing unusual). The index matched the word in both. A reader sees the difference immediately – the index does not."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "BEGIN\n  CTX_DOC.SET_KEY_TYPE('PRIMARY_KEY');\nEND;\n/"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT s.report_id,\n       CTX_DOC.SNIPPET('SYMPTOM_REPORTS_TXT_IDX', s.report_id, 'Plopp') AS snippet\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\nAND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Plopp') > 0\nORDER  BY s.report_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. Precise, but blind to synonyms\n\nPatients rarely use the same words: one hears a \"Knall\", another feels something \"schnappen\".\n\n**Question:** How much does the result depend on choosing the right words?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A keyword search finds words, not meanings. Oracle Text widens the search in several ways, but someone has to know the words:\n\n| Query | Technique |\n|---|---|\n| `Plopp` | exact word, also inside *Plopp-Geräusch* |\n| `?Plop` | fuzzy: spelling variants and typing errors |\n| `Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen` | synonyms, written by hand |\n| `$verdrehen` | German stem: *verdreht*, *verdrehen* |\n| `NEAR((Knie, wackelig), 5)` | both words at most 5 words apart |\n\nThe query counts the hits and how many of them were cruciate ligament ruptures (`vkb_ruptur`).\n\n**Result:**\n\n- *Plopp* and the fuzzy *?Plop* find the same 12 reports, 7 of them cruciate ligament ruptures – fuzzy matching only helps when people misspell.\n- The hand-written synonyms find 22 reports, 20 of them cruciate ligament ruptures: three times as many cases, and far more precise.\n- The stem *$verdrehen* finds 58 reports, but only 18 ruptures – a twist is the mechanism of many knee injuries, not a diagnosis.\n- Jonas' own words *Knie* … *wackelig* appear close together in only 2 reports – other people describe instability differently.\n\nThe best query is the one with the right synonyms. But they only work because someone knew them – step 4 shows where this knowledge can come from: the knowledge graph."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT 'Plopp' AS text_query, 'exact word' AS technique,\n       COUNT(*) AS hits, COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END) AS acl_ruptures\nFROM   symptom_reports s LEFT JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir' AND CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Plopp') > 0\nUNION ALL\nSELECT '?Plop', 'fuzzy',\n       COUNT(*), COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END)\nFROM   symptom_reports s LEFT JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir' AND CONTAINS(s.report_text, '?Plop') > 0\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen', 'synonyms by hand',\n       COUNT(*), COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END)\nFROM   symptom_reports s LEFT JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir' AND CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Knall OR geknallt OR Schnappen') > 0\nUNION ALL\nSELECT '$verdrehen', 'stemming',\n       COUNT(*), COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END)\nFROM   symptom_reports s LEFT JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir' AND CONTAINS(s.report_text, '$verdrehen') > 0\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'NEAR((Knie, wackelig), 5)', 'proximity',\n       COUNT(*), COUNT(CASE WHEN d.diagnosis_code = 'vkb_ruptur' THEN 1 END)\nFROM   symptom_reports s LEFT JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir' AND CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'NEAR((Knie, wackelig), 5)') > 0;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. Does the query use the text index?\n\nWith 1,500 reports every query is fast. With millions it has to be the index.\n\n**Question:** Does the database answer `CONTAINS` with the text index?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`EXPLAIN PLAN` and `DBMS_XPLAN.DISPLAY` show the execution plan. The operation `DOMAIN INDEX` on `SYMPTOM_REPORTS_TXT_IDX` confirms that `CONTAINS` is answered by the text index.\n\n**Result:** step 10 is `DOMAIN INDEX` on `SYMPTOM_REPORTS_TXT_IDX` with the predicate `CONTAINS(REPORT_TEXT, 'Plopp', 1) > 0` – the text index finds the hits, and only those rows are read from the table (`TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID`). There is no full table scan. The `PX` steps come from Autonomous Database, which runs the query in parallel; they do not change the access path. With 1,459 reports this hardly matters – with millions it is the difference between milliseconds and minutes."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "EXPLAIN PLAN FOR\nSELECT s.report_id, SCORE(1) AS score\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nWHERE  CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Plopp', 1) > 0\nORDER  BY score DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT plan_table_output\nFROM   TABLE(DBMS_XPLAN.DISPLAY(format => 'BASIC +PREDICATE'));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. Vector search: the reports closest to Jonas' report\n\nNow the same question by meaning instead of by words.\n\n**Question:** Which reports are closest to Jonas' report in meaning?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`VECTOR_DISTANCE(…, COSINE)` compares the embedding of every report with Jonas' embedding; the smaller the distance, the more similar the meaning. `COSINE` is the metric the model was trained with. Jonas' vector comes from a scalar subquery, so no join is needed. Without a vector index the search is exact.\n\n**Result:** the ten closest reports spread over five diagnoses: medial ligament strains (*Innenbandzerrung*) and medial meniscus lesions (*Innenmeniskusläsion*) three times each, contusions (*Knieprellung*) twice, one sprain and one cruciate ligament rupture. They share Jonas' words and setting – descent, hiking, *tut beim Gehen weh* – rather than his findings. The only rupture (rank 4) is the only one with rapid swelling and a knee that gives way. Not one of the ten mentions a \"Plopp\"."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT s.report_id,\n       ROUND(VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, (SELECT j.report_embedding FROM symptom_reports j WHERE j.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'), COSINE), 4) AS distance,\n       d.diagnosis_label,\n       s.report_text\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\nORDER  BY VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, (SELECT j.report_embedding FROM symptom_reports j WHERE j.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'), COSINE)\nFETCH  FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 8. What would a similarity app recommend?\n\nA symptom checker built only on similarity would stop here and give advice.\n\n**Question:** What would it recommend, based on the 20 closest cases?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The 20 nearest reports vote: diagnoses and the medications recommended for them.\n\n**Result:** the vote of the 20 nearest reports goes to the sprain (*Kniedistorsion*, 7), followed by the medial ligament strain (5). Only 3 are cruciate ligament ruptures. Both front runners are treated the same way: diclofenac gel and ibuprofen 400 mg. A pure similarity app would say: *\"Just a sprain – rest, cool, pain gel.\"*"
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH nearest AS (\n  SELECT s.report_id\n  FROM   symptom_reports s\n  WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n  ORDER  BY VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, (SELECT j.report_embedding FROM symptom_reports j WHERE j.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'), COSINE)\n  FETCH  FIRST 20 ROWS ONLY\n)\nSELECT d.diagnosis_label,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT n.report_id)                                AS reports,\n       LISTAGG(DISTINCT m.medication_text, '; ' ON OVERFLOW TRUNCATE)\n         WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY m.medication_text)                AS recommended_medications\nFROM   nearest n\nJOIN   diagnoses d ON d.report_id = n.report_id\nLEFT   JOIN medication_recommendations m ON m.diagnosis_id = d.diagnosis_id\nGROUP  BY d.diagnosis_label\nORDER  BY reports DESC;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 9. Search with any text: embedding at query time\n\nJonas could just as well have written \"es hat geknallt, das Knie gibt nach\".\n\n**Question:** Does the search by meaning also work for a new text with different words?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The question does not have to be in the table. `VECTOR_EMBEDDING(ALL_MINILM_L12_V2 USING '<text>' AS data)` calculates the embedding of a new text in the database, with the same model as the data. Here a short text without the word \"Plopp\" but with synonyms.\n\n`<=>` is the short form of `VECTOR_DISTANCE(…, COSINE)`. `FETCH APPROX` asks for an approximate search; without a vector index the database searches exactly.\n\n**Result:** with the words *geknallt* and *gibt nach*, 7 of the 10 closest reports are cruciate ligament ruptures – four of them say *\"Es hat im Knie geknallt\"*, one *\"Plopp\"* and one *\"ein Geräusch wie ein reißendes Seil\"*. The three sprains share only *verdreht* and the descent. The vector search is not wrong; it follows the words it is given. Jonas wrote *wackelig* and *geschwollen* – and got milder injuries. Whoever knows the telling findings gets the ruptures. Step 4 shows where this knowledge can come from."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH question AS (\n  SELECT VECTOR_EMBEDDING(ALL_MINILM_L12_V2 USING 'Knie verdreht, es hat geknallt, das Knie gibt nach' AS data) AS v\n)\nSELECT s.report_id,\n       ROUND(s.report_embedding <=> (SELECT q.v FROM question q), 4) AS distance,\n       d.diagnosis_label,\n       s.report_text\nFROM   symptom_reports s\nLEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\nWHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\nORDER  BY s.report_embedding <=> (SELECT q.v FROM question q)\nFETCH  APPROX FIRST 10 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 10. Two lists that contradict each other\n\nTwo searches, two answers – and they do not agree.\n\n**Question:** Do keyword and meaning point to the same diagnosis?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The top 10 of both searches side by side, rank by rank. `ROW_NUMBER` numbers each list; the `FULL OUTER JOIN` on the rank puts them next to each other.\n\n**Result:** the two lists have not a single report in common. The keyword list shows 6 cruciate ligament ruptures among its first 10 (7 of all 12 hits); because all hits have the same score, the order within the list is only the report ID. The vector list shows one rupture and otherwise milder injuries – strains, contusions, meniscus lesions; in the vote of the 20 nearest reports the sprain wins. Which one is right? Similar is not the same – step 4 checks the candidates against medical knowledge."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH keyword_hits AS (\n  SELECT s.report_id, d.diagnosis_label, SCORE(1) AS score\n  FROM   symptom_reports s\n  LEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n  WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n  AND    CONTAINS(s.report_text, 'Plopp', 1) > 0\n),\nkeyword_list AS (\n  SELECT ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC, report_id) AS rank_no, report_id, diagnosis_label\n  FROM   keyword_hits\n),\nvector_list AS (\n  SELECT ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY VECTOR_DISTANCE(s.report_embedding, (SELECT j.report_embedding FROM symptom_reports j WHERE j.report_id = 'SR-2026-11-16-0001'), COSINE)) AS rank_no,\n         s.report_id,\n         d.diagnosis_label\n  FROM   symptom_reports s\n  LEFT   JOIN diagnoses d ON d.report_id = s.report_id\n  WHERE  s.source = 'fhir'\n)\nSELECT rank_no,\n       k.report_id        AS keyword_report,\n       k.diagnosis_label  AS keyword_diagnosis,\n       v.report_id        AS vector_report,\n       v.diagnosis_label  AS vector_diagnosis\nFROM   keyword_list k\nFULL   OUTER JOIN vector_list v USING (rank_no)\nWHERE  rank_no <= 10\nORDER  BY rank_no;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- **Full text** is precise and explainable, but only finds the words someone types. \"Plopp\" leads to cruciate ligament ruptures.\n- **Vector search** finds similar meaning without the same words – and ranks reports that sound like Jonas first, not those with his findings. The vote of the nearest reports says: sprain.\n- A **hybrid vector index** combines both in one index and one ranking (theory part; see **11d**).\n- *\"Now we have a suspicion. But do we know?\"* Next: **11e** – the knowledge graph."
    languageId: markdown
